<!-- 6. FAULT LINES -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<section id="fault-lines">
  <h2><span class="sg-section-num">Section 6</span>The Five Fault Lines</h2>
  <p>The roundtable is organized around five interlocking tensions. For each, we provide the core question, which speakers are most directly engaged, and what to think about beforehand.</p>

  <div class="sg-callout sg-callout-purple">
    <div class="sg-callout-label">1. Evidence &amp; Deployment</div>
    <p><strong>Core question:</strong> What counts as sufficient evidence to deploy AI in clinical care?</p>
    <p>Aphinyanaphongs has deployed 31+ models; Chunara has shown that similar models can encode bias in safety-net hospitals. The threshold for &ldquo;enough&rdquo; evidence looks very different depending on whether you prioritize speed of impact or equity of outcomes.</p>
    <p><strong>Think about:</strong> How many patients need to benefit before deployment is justified? Who decides, and how do you account for patients who might be harmed?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-callout sg-callout-purple">
    <div class="sg-callout-label">2. The Body as Data</div>
    <p><strong>Core question:</strong> What is gained and what is lost when the patient becomes a data substrate?</p>
    <p>From Choudhury&rsquo;s smartphone stress sensors to Crary&rsquo;s brain tissue slides to Brandes&rsquo; protein sequences, AI increasingly &ldquo;knows&rdquo; the patient through computational representations rather than clinical encounters.</p>
    <p><strong>Think about:</strong> Does an AI model that reads your genome &ldquo;know&rdquo; you? Does it know you differently than a model that reads your clinical notes? What about one that listens to your voice for signs of stress?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-callout sg-callout-purple">
    <div class="sg-callout-label">3. Equity &amp; Scale</div>
    <p><strong>Core question:</strong> How do we prevent AI from scaling existing health disparities?</p>
    <p>Chunara&rsquo;s research shows bias is not hypothetical &mdash; it is measurable in real systems. Choudhury studies whether digital mental health tools work equitably across populations. Aphinyanaphongs deploys at scale across a major urban system.</p>
    <p><strong>Think about:</strong> If a model improves outcomes for 80% of patients but worsens outcomes for a vulnerable 20%, should it be deployed? Who makes that call?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-callout sg-callout-purple">
    <div class="sg-callout-label">4. Trust Across Scales</div>
    <p><strong>Core question:</strong> Can a single framework for &ldquo;trust in medical AI&rdquo; span from molecules to health systems?</p>
    <p>Trust means something different when a clinician trusts a protein variant prediction (Brandes), an AI pathology reading (Crary), a smartphone behavioral assessment (Choudhury), an EHR mortality alert (Aphinyanaphongs), or a fairness audit (Chunara).</p>
    <p><strong>Think about:</strong> Is &ldquo;trust&rdquo; even the right word? When you &ldquo;trust&rdquo; a molecular prediction, is that the same kind of trust as when you trust a doctor?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-callout sg-callout-purple">
    <div class="sg-callout-label">5. Governance from the Inside</div>
    <p><strong>Core question:</strong> What happens when the people building AI are also the ones governing it?</p>
    <p>Without a dedicated regulator on the panel, governance questions fall to the builders. Brandes writes on data governance, Chunara on responsible AI, Aphinyanaphongs on institutional AI policy, Choudhury on navigating FDA/IRB processes as an entrepreneur.</p>
    <p><strong>Think about:</strong> Can builders effectively regulate themselves? What does &ldquo;governance&rdquo; look like when it comes from practitioners rather than external regulators? Is this better or worse?</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 7. TENSIONS TO WATCH -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<section id="tensions">
  <h2><span class="sg-section-num">Section 7</span>Key Tensions to Watch</h2>
  <p>The most generative conversations at Helix roundtables emerge from productive disagreements between speakers. Here are the pairings to watch:</p>

  <div class="sg-tension-pair">
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Aphinyanaphongs</strong><br>The deployer: &ldquo;We have 31 models live. The evidence supports action.&rdquo;</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-vs">vs.</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Chunara</strong><br>The auditor: &ldquo;Similar models encode bias in safety-net hospitals. We must audit first.&rdquo;</div>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-tension-pair">
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Choudhury</strong><br>Patient-facing AI: wearables, apps, direct-to-patient sensing</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-vs">vs.</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Aphinyanaphongs</strong><br>Clinician-facing AI: EHR alerts, predictive models, workflow tools</div>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-tension-pair">
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Brandes</strong><br>Foundational science: protein language models, pre-clinical tools</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-vs">vs.</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Aphinyanaphongs</strong><br>Operational deployment: the &ldquo;last mile&rdquo; from model to bedside</div>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-tension-pair">
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Brandes</strong><br>Molecular scale: AI reads protein sequences</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-vs">vs.</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Crary</strong><br>Tissue scale: AI reads brain slides</div>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-tension-pair">
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Choudhury</strong><br>Sensing as empowerment: early detection, patient agency</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-vs">vs.</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Chunara</strong><br>Sensing as surveillance: who is monitored, who benefits?</div>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-tension-pair">
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Aphinyanaphongs</strong><br>Near-term: maximize clinical impact now</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-vs">vs.</div>
    <div class="sg-tension-side"><strong>Brandes</strong><br>Long-term: existential AI safety risks demand caution</div>
  </div>

  <div class="sg-callout sg-callout-red">
    <div class="sg-callout-label">The Central Debate</div>
    <p>The <strong>Aphinyanaphongs&ndash;Chunara</strong> tension is the panel&rsquo;s defining axis. Both are at NYU. Both care about patient outcomes. They approach deployment from opposite directions &mdash; scale versus scrutiny, action versus audit. The question of whether to deploy first and audit later, or audit first and deploy later, is the roundtable&rsquo;s most consequential operational disagreement. Listen for it early and throughout.</p>
  </div>
</section>

